>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWe can compute $\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}$ by applying the chain rule.\n\nLet $y=Ax-b\\in {R}^{3}.$ Then, $L(A)={y}^{T}y$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}={\\sum }_{k=1}^{3}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n{∇}_{y}L={∇}_{y}({y}^{T}y)=2y,\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}=2{y}_{k}.\n$$\n\nWriting out $y=Ax-b$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\n{y}_{i}={\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{a}_{ij}{x}_{j}-{b}_{i},\n$$\n\nwe have \n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}={x}_{j}$ if $i=k$, and\n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}=0$ if $i\\ne k.$\n\nThis means that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}} & ={\\sum }_{k=1}^{3}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{i}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =2{y}_{i}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =2{y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $(i,j)$th entry of ${∇}_{A}L$ is $2$ times the $i$th entry of $y$ multiplied by the $j$th entry of $x$, so\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=2y\\cdot {x}^{T}.\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating ${∇}_{A}L$ at $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{A}L & =2y\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =2(Ax-b)\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =2([\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2([\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -32 & -32 \\\\ 16 & 16\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0993d2df-afb2-5918-abc9-0085f57ccea0" "answer-field-0b2f56de-5698-580f-a95c-fdc0907069d0" "answer-field-f07a1f18-7d90-5690-825e-3d3440849512"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0993d2df-afb2-5918-abc9-0085f57ccea0" :answer-field/id #uuid "0993d2df-afb2-5918-abc9-0085f57ccea0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-d5fa7b5b-ee3b-5ec5-82f7-0d5489952a88" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d5fa7b5b-ee3b-5ec5-82f7-0d5489952a88"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d5fa7b5b-ee3b-5ec5-82f7-0d5489952a88" :answer/id #uuid "d5fa7b5b-ee3b-5ec5-82f7-0d5489952a88" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0b2f56de-5698-580f-a95c-fdc0907069d0" :answer-field/id #uuid "0b2f56de-5698-580f-a95c-fdc0907069d0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-f04b7aa7-21c0-58cb-a044-6cd8f614ccdf" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f04b7aa7-21c0-58cb-a044-6cd8f614ccdf"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f04b7aa7-21c0-58cb-a044-6cd8f614ccdf" :answer/id #uuid "f04b7aa7-21c0-58cb-a044-6cd8f614ccdf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-32" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f07a1f18-7d90-5690-825e-3d3440849512" :answer-field/id #uuid "f07a1f18-7d90-5690-825e-3d3440849512" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-df1c126c-4286-537f-a872-e032f4f131ea" :answer-field/choices ["answer-df1c126c-4286-537f-a872-e032f4f131ea"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-df1c126c-4286-537f-a872-e032f4f131ea" :answer/id #uuid "df1c126c-4286-537f-a872-e032f4f131ea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e1b5136b-802c-5748-926e-3ce486df5870" :question/id #uuid "e1b5136b-802c-5748-926e-3ce486df5870" :question/math-academy-id "q-269472" :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2\\times 3}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)=(2Ax-b)^{T}(2Ax-b),$ where\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]?$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">\n${∇}_{A}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">$-240$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-2140$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWe can compute $\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}$ by applying the chain rule.\n\nLet $y=2Ax-b\\in {R}^{2}.$ Then, $L(A)={y}^{T}y$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}={\\sum }_{k=1}^{2}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n{∇}_{y}L={∇}_{y}({y}^{T}y)=2y,\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}=2{y}_{k}.\n$$\n\nWriting out $y=2Ax-b$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\n{y}_{i}=2{\\sum }_{j=1}^{3}{a}_{ij}{x}_{j}-{b}_{i},\n$$\n\nwe have \n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}=2{x}_{j}$ if $i=k$, and\n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}=0$ if $i\\ne k.$\n\nThis means that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}} & ={\\sum }_{k=1}^{2}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{i}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =2{y}_{i}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =2{y}_{i}\\cdot 2{x}_{j} \\\\  & =4{y}_{i}{x}_{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n The $(i,j)$th entry of ${∇}_{A}L$ is $4$ times the $i$th entry of $y$ multiplied by the $j$th entry of $x$, so\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=4y\\cdot {x}^{T}.\n$$\n\n Finally, evaluating ${∇}_{A}L$ at $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{A}L & =4y\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =4(2Ax-b)\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =4(2[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}10 & 10 & -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =4([\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 110\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}10 & 10 & -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}48 \\\\ 428\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}10 & 10 & -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}480 & 480 & -240 \\\\ 4280 & 4280 & -2140\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f55e12fe-6e05-5d4a-be95-d4e0ff57c8bd" "answer-field-9b35ca60-322b-555a-a5bd-d55c7f80941e" "answer-field-e8bb2f47-6b22-5ef2-a095-870537c2a058" "answer-field-4a1b8af5-7b32-59e0-af44-041131fdb958"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f55e12fe-6e05-5d4a-be95-d4e0ff57c8bd" :answer-field/id #uuid "f55e12fe-6e05-5d4a-be95-d4e0ff57c8bd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-5fe59fe2-e68f-5e11-84b7-67ac73d71ee2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5fe59fe2-e68f-5e11-84b7-67ac73d71ee2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5fe59fe2-e68f-5e11-84b7-67ac73d71ee2" :answer/id #uuid "5fe59fe2-e68f-5e11-84b7-67ac73d71ee2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "480" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9b35ca60-322b-555a-a5bd-d55c7f80941e" :answer-field/id #uuid "9b35ca60-322b-555a-a5bd-d55c7f80941e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-847371f3-c4cc-51af-87c4-80f81681286d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-847371f3-c4cc-51af-87c4-80f81681286d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-847371f3-c4cc-51af-87c4-80f81681286d" :answer/id #uuid "847371f3-c4cc-51af-87c4-80f81681286d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "480" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e8bb2f47-6b22-5ef2-a095-870537c2a058" :answer-field/id #uuid "e8bb2f47-6b22-5ef2-a095-870537c2a058" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-e4b7f285-21bd-5c63-961f-8c698aa411e9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e4b7f285-21bd-5c63-961f-8c698aa411e9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e4b7f285-21bd-5c63-961f-8c698aa411e9" :answer/id #uuid "e4b7f285-21bd-5c63-961f-8c698aa411e9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4280" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4a1b8af5-7b32-59e0-af44-041131fdb958" :answer-field/id #uuid "4a1b8af5-7b32-59e0-af44-041131fdb958" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-ccc7cd39-2b31-5e60-9f7f-6d6dbdd6fcd6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ccc7cd39-2b31-5e60-9f7f-6d6dbdd6fcd6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ccc7cd39-2b31-5e60-9f7f-6d6dbdd6fcd6" :answer/id #uuid "ccc7cd39-2b31-5e60-9f7f-6d6dbdd6fcd6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4280" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186204585 :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{3\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)=(3Ax-2b)^{T}(3Ax-2b),$ where\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]?$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"3\">\n${∇}_{A}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td rowspan=\"3\" width=\"8px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"45px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"45px\">$-96$</td>\n<td rowspan=\"3\" width=\"8px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$864$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWe can compute $\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}$ by applying the chain rule.\n\nLet $y=3Ax-2b\\in {R}^{3}.$ Then, $L(A)={y}^{T}y$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}={\\sum }_{k=1}^{3}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n{∇}_{y}L={∇}_{y}({y}^{T}y)=2y,\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}=2{y}_{k}.\n$$\n\nWriting out $y=3Ax-2b$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\n{y}_{i}=3{\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{a}_{ij}{x}_{j}-2{b}_{i},\n$$\n\nwe have \n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}=3{x}_{j}$ if $i=k$, and\n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}=0$ if $i\\ne k.$\n\nThis means that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}} & ={\\sum }_{k=1}^{3}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{i}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =2{y}_{i}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =2{y}_{i}\\cdot 3{x}_{j} \\\\  & =6{y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $(i,j)$th entry of ${∇}_{A}L$ is $6$ times the $i$th entry of $y$ multiplied by the $j$th entry of $x$, so\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=6y\\cdot {x}^{T}.\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating ${∇}_{A}L$ at $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{A}L & =6y\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =6(3Ax-2b)\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =6(3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =6(3[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-24 \\\\ 132 \\\\ 216\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -96 \\\\ 0 & 528 \\\\ 0 & 864\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186203439 :answer-field/correct "answer-1ef11ea8-6ba0-5016-814e-da90180fe8bc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1ef11ea8-6ba0-5016-814e-da90180fe8bc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1ef11ea8-6ba0-5016-814e-da90180fe8bc" :answer/id #uuid "1ef11ea8-6ba0-5016-814e-da90180fe8bc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186203438 :answer-field/correct "answer-ff0f6ad8-5133-5024-a578-c55897acba97" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ff0f6ad8-5133-5024-a578-c55897acba97"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ff0f6ad8-5133-5024-a578-c55897acba97" :answer/id #uuid "ff0f6ad8-5133-5024-a578-c55897acba97" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "528" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186203440 :answer-field/correct "answer-6e194e37-0807-5200-a69e-400c7a758e74" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6e194e37-0807-5200-a69e-400c7a758e74"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6e194e37-0807-5200-a69e-400c7a758e74" :answer/id #uuid "6e194e37-0807-5200-a69e-400c7a758e74" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186205186 :tutorial/content "In previous lessons, we defined the gradient of a scalar-valued function with respect to a vector. In this lesson, we extend this idea to functions where the input is a *matrix* and the output is a scalar.\n\nSuppose we have a function $f: \\mathbb{R}^{m \\times n} \\to \\mathbb{R}$ that maps a matrix $A \\in \\mathbb{R}^{m \\times n}$ to a scalar value $f(A)$. Just like with vector inputs, we define the **gradient of $f$ with respect to $A$** as the matrix of partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to each individual entry of $A{:}$\n\n![](images/0b/0b3ce184c982fe85eb25f79161431b7620227b96909d87998fa82efbb469423c.svg)\n\nThis gradient has the same shape as the matrix $A.$ It tells us how sensitive the output $f(A)$ is to changes in each element $a_{ij}$.\n\nLet’s apply this to a concrete example. Suppose we define the function\n\n$$\nf(X) = \\ln(\\det(X))\n$$\n\nfor a square matrix $X \\in \\mathbb{R}^{2 \\times 2}$. Let's find the gradient of $f$ with respect to $X$ and evaluate it at a specific point.\n\nFirst, we write the matrix $X$ as\n![](images/67/67c8cc92409c6bd1595fda93c8711ce91a40d220526cc52ee9a8f4d3842f7354.svg)\nThen, the determinant of $X$ is\n\n$$\n\\det(X) = x_{11}x_{22} - x_{12}x_{21}.\n$$\n\nSo, the function becomes\n\n$$\nf(X) = \\ln(x_{11}x_{22} - x_{12}x_{21}).\n$$\n\nTo find the gradient, we differentiate $f$ with respect to each element of $X.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{X}f(X) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{12}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{22}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{11}}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{12}}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{21}}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{22}}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}))\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}_{22}}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}} & -\\frac{{x}_{21}}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}} \\\\ -\\frac{{x}_{12}}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}} & \\frac{{x}_{11}}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}}[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{22} & -{x}_{21} \\\\ -{x}_{12} & {x}_{11}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{det(X)}[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{22} & -{x}_{21} \\\\ -{x}_{12} & {x}_{11}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, suppose we wish to evaluate this gradient at\n![](images/93/93200d9d52e6a58a52e4b9f65db6bc13ded0c6e17dbb8fc0381ae40081932ff9.svg)\nFirst, we compute the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(X) & =3\\cdot (-2)-1\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-6-5 \\\\  & =-11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our values into our gradient. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{X}f(X) & =-\\frac{1}{11}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{11}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis result tells us how the scalar output $f(X) = \\ln(\\det(X))$ would change with respect to small changes in each entry of the matrix $X$. Each element in the gradient matrix captures that local rate of change." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186205192 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a scalar-valued function defined by\n\n$$\nf = \\mathbf{y}^T A \\mathbf{x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{y} \\in \\mathbb{R}^m$, $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$, and $A \\in \\mathbb{R}^{m \\times n}$.\n\nWe want to compute the gradient of $f$ with respect to the matrix $A$. The result turns out to be\n\n$$\n\\nabla_A f = \\mathbf{y} \\cdot \\mathbf{x}^T.\n$$\n\nLet’s verify this by working component-wise. \n\nFirst, note that we can write the product $\\mathbf y^T A \\mathbf x$ as a double summation as follows:\n\n$$\nf = \\mathbf y^T A \\mathbf x = \\sum_{k=1}^m \\sum_{l=1}^n y_k A_{kl} x_l.\n$$\n\nIn a previous lesson, we saw how to do this for $\\mathbf x^T A \\mathbf x$. The idea here is similar.\n\nNow, we differentiate this summation with respect to $A_{ij}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial A_{ij}} \n= \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial A_{ij}} \\left( \\sum_{k=1}^m \\sum_{l=1}^n y_k A_{kl} x_l \\right)\n$$\n\nSince only the term where $k = i$ and $l = j$ depends on $A_{ij}$, all other terms vanish. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial A_{ij}} \n= y_i x_j.\n$$\n\nThis tells us that the entry in the $i$th row and $j$th column of the gradient matrix is $y_i x_j$. Hence, the full gradient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\nabla_A f = \\mathbf{y} \\cdot \\mathbf{x}^T.\n$$\n\nThis result is an example of an outer product. The gradient of $\\mathbf{y}^T A \\mathbf{x}$ with respect to the matrix $A$ is given by multiplying the column vector $\\mathbf{y}$ by the row vector $\\mathbf{x}^T$, resulting in a matrix of the same shape as $A$.\n\nLet’s look at a few examples to see this in action." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186205195 :tutorial/content "When working with scalar-valued functions defined on matrices, it's often helpful to compute gradients using component-wise differentiation and the chain rule. This approach breaks down the computation into simpler intermediate steps and allows us to clearly see the dependencies.\n\nConsider a *scalar* function $L(\\mathbf A),$ defined as\n\n$$\nL(\\mathbf{A}) = \\frac{1}{2} \\left( \\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b} \\right)^T \\left( \\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b} \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{A} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{2 \\times 3}$, $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$, and $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2.$\n\nSuppose we want to compute the gradient $\\nabla_{\\mathbf{A}} L$, which is a matrix of the same shape as $\\mathbf{A}$. The entry in the $i$th row and $j$th column of the gradient matrix is given by \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial L}{\\partial a_{ij}}.\n$$\n\nTo simplify the differentiation, we define $\\mathbf y \\in\\mathbb R^{2\\times 1}$ as \n![](images/d1/d14a8e7752fdc2f20c9c325c5a0076d79a86ccd2a0c46d6b353ce53c3f1561d7.svg)\n\nThen, we can write $L$ in terms of $\\mathbf y$ as \n\n$$\nL = \\frac{1}{2} \\mathbf{y}^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nTo compute $\\dfrac{\\partial L}{\\partial a_{ij}}$, we apply the multivariable chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial a_{ij}} = \\sum_{k=1}^2 \\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial y_k} \\cdot \\frac{\\partial y_k}{\\partial a_{ij}}\n$$\n\nNote that the summation is from $k=1$ to $k=2$ because $\\mathbf y$ has two components, and we're differentiating with respect to each of them.\n\nNow, let's analyze the two factors of the product that appear in this summation.\n\n- We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{y}L & ={∇}_{y}(\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{T}y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {∇}_{y}({y}^{T}y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial y_k} = y_k.\n$$\n\n- Writing $\\mathbf{y} = \\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b}$ in component form\n\n$$\ny_i = \\sum_{j=1}^3 a_{ij} x_j - b_i,\n$$\n\nwe have\n![](images/76/761731b884eee7282e01bda6964861fc8f3d4a3a44bfec09fda478170982b56a.svg)\nOnly the $k$th row of $\\mathbf{A}$ affects $y_k$.\n\nPutting our two results together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}} & ={\\sum }_{k=1}^{2}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{i}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & ={y}_{i}{x}_{j},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbecause all terms vanish except the one where $k=i$.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf{A}} L = \\mathbf{y} \\cdot \\mathbf{x}^T.\n$$\n\nIn the next example, we will use this formula to compute the gradient for specific vectors." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186204182 :activity/expected-seconds 1020.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}] "assertions" [[17592186203857 :question/problem "Consider the function $f: \\mathbb{R}^{2 \\times 2} \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(X) = (\\det(X))^2.\n$$\n\n \n\nWhat is the gradient of $f$ with respect to $X$ at ![](images/70/70a078e9f26d91a1beb588e88c812057b85d31586576c94eaa510dedba7da2f0.svg)"] [17592186203857 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of a function $f: \\mathbb{R}^{m \\times n} \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}$ with respect to a matrix $X \\in \\mathbb{R}^{m \\times n}$ is\n![](images/26/26b51d20ec90168f9bf7602ed9e5f05cb4a5fb1421bb52fd0042ff81faa5f8ab.svg)\n\nIn our case, writing out the function $f(X)$ where ![](images/db/dbd3e061ec5b3c6e148c347b0bf829391218f883ba4e6b1271bf8f65fb08d38b.svg), we get\n\n$$\nf(X) = (\\det(X))^2 = (x_{11}x_{22} - x_{12}x_{21})^2.\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,{∇}_{X}f(X) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{12}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{22}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{11}}({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})^{2} & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{12}}({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{21}}({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})^{2} & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{22}}({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})^{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})\\cdot {x}_{22} & 2({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})\\cdot (-{x}_{21}) \\\\ 2({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})\\cdot (-{x}_{12}) & 2({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})\\cdot {x}_{11}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{22} & -{x}_{21} \\\\ -{x}_{12} & {x}_{11}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2det(X)[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{22} & -{x}_{21} \\\\ -{x}_{12} & {x}_{11}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at ![](images/79/790980ee64747a4ef3becd801431083d66839972cb271207807c43c53cf166fa.svg) we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{X}f(X) & =2(-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 10-(-4)\\cdot \\frac{3}{2})[\\begin{aligned}10 & 4 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2(-5-(-6))[\\begin{aligned}10 & 4 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}20 & 8 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186203871 :question/problem "Consider the function $L: \\mathbb{R}^{2 \\times 3} \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}$ defined by $L(\\mathbf{A}) = \\mathbf{y}^T\\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{x}$ where \n![](images/1d/1ddd8273660c1a84671e442af51d78ecc3df160fc0b807695c12297300118aa8.svg)\nWhat is $\\nabla_{\\mathbf{A}} L$ at \n![](images/c6/c66b8dc8fde116a844d833de35034c534b650a9b7f788eef8366e770f6923380.svg)"] [17592186203871 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $\\mathbf{A}$ is a matrix of the same shape as $\\mathbf{A}$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial L}{\\partial a_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $\\mathbf{A}.$\n\nWriting out $L$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\nL = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^2 \\sum\\limits_{j=1}^3 a_{ij} y_i x_j,\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial L}{\\partial a_{ij}} = y_i x_j.\n$$\n\nThus, $\\nabla_{\\mathbf{A}} L = \\mathbf{y} \\cdot \\mathbf{x}^T.$\n\nEvaluating at the given values ![](images/d8/d8a406621f1e60112d233f79cdde2e1405681554510ac4ea6af68409b4af4676.svg) and ![](images/df/dfa340dc46ddf622d0a16fdf8335a5183786294d395201e48f9adee4182bd91d.svg) we get\n\n![](images/b6/b6250171f2d99fb806cbb449b18f3a9c0513d8eac898d256d020e0b2cb986f8b.svg)"] [17592186203866 :question/problem "Consider the function $L: \\mathbb{R}^{2 \\times 3}   \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}$ defined by $L(\\mathbf{A}) = \\dfrac{1}{2}(\\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b})^T(\\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b}),$ where\n![](images/51/518bfea30c779d3e9dc1ab6b77b0ddaa9b938b56b0bb3b9e68d6c9b9cdae5666.svg)\nWhat is $\\nabla_{\\mathbf{A}} L$ at \n![](images/a1/a1e2b39a295cebfd8425e8ab73e4d915233ca3afbd4141c54332c38f1091be15.svg)"] [17592186203866 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $\\mathbf{A}$ is a matrix of the same shape as $\\mathbf{A}$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial L}{\\partial a_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $\\mathbf{A}.$\n\nWe can compute $\\dfrac{\\partial L}{\\partial a_{ij}}$ by applying the chain rule.\n\nLet $\\mathbf{y} = \\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2.$ Then, $L(\\mathbf{A}) = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\mathbf{y}^T\\mathbf{y}$ and\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial L}{\\partial a_{ij}} = \\sum_{k=1}^2 \\dfrac{\\partial L}{\\partial y_k} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial y_k}{\\partial a_{ij}}\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf{y}} L = \\nabla_{\\mathbf{y}} \\bigg( \\dfrac{1}{2}\\mathbf{y}^T\\mathbf{y} \\bigg) = \\mathbf{y},\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial L}{\\partial y_k} = y_k.\n$$\n\nWriting out $\\mathbf{y} = \\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b}$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\ny_i = \\sum\\limits_{j=1}^3 (a_{ij} x_j - b_i),\n$$\n\nwe have \n\n- $\\dfrac{\\partial y_k}{\\partial a_{ij}} = x_j$ if $i = k$, and\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\partial y_k}{\\partial a_{ij}} = 0$ if $i \\neq k.$\n\nThis means that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}} & ={\\sum }_{k=1}^{2}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{i}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & ={y}_{i}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & ={y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $(i,j)$th entry of $\\nabla_{\\mathbf{A}} L$ is the $i$th entry of $\\mathbf{y}$ multiplied by the $j$th entry of $\\mathbf{x}$, so\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf{A}} L = \\mathbf{y} \\cdot \\mathbf{x}^T.\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating $\\nabla_{\\mathbf{A}} L$ at ![](images/ee/ee9b9f891c92caf4229e8e92c20031fe7ba9e1cc0472105caf5a6cfaa8541a27.svg) we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{A}L & =y\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =(Ax-b)\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =([\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186204564 :question/problem "Consider the function $f:{R}^{2\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(X)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(det(X)).\n$$\n\n \nWhat is the gradient of $f$ with respect to $X$ at $X=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]?$"] [17592186204564 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of a function $f:{R}^{m\\times n}\\to R$ with respect to a matrix $X\\in {R}^{m\\times n}$ is\n\n$$\n{∇}_{X}f(X)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{12}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{22}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{mn}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, writing out the function $f(X)$ where $X=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11} & {x}_{12} \\\\ {x}_{21} & {x}_{22}\\end{aligned}]\\in {R}^{2\\times 2}$, we get\n\n$$\nf(X)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(det(X))=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}).\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,{∇}_{X}f(X) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{12}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{22}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{11}}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{12}}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{21}}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{22}}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}))\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-{x}_{22}}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}} & \\frac{{x}_{21}}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}} \\\\ \\frac{{x}_{12}}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}} & \\frac{-{x}_{11}}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $X=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{X}f(X)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{4-1} & \\frac{1}{4-1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4-1} & -\\frac{2}{4-1}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [17592186204564 :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy] ["answer-dd2c45f0-ab57-5f4c-af72-1b497341005f" :answer/id #uuid "dd2c45f0-ab57-5f4c-af72-1b497341005f"] ["answer-dd2c45f0-ab57-5f4c-af72-1b497341005f" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-dd2c45f0-ab57-5f4c-af72-1b497341005f" :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]"] ["answer-23f517c8-02d6-5cbc-bbf1-171ed91219de" :answer/id #uuid "23f517c8-02d6-5cbc-bbf1-171ed91219de"] ["answer-23f517c8-02d6-5cbc-bbf1-171ed91219de" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-23f517c8-02d6-5cbc-bbf1-171ed91219de" :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["answer-efa876a3-a667-54e0-abff-4b40c1125d55" :answer/id #uuid "efa876a3-a667-54e0-abff-4b40c1125d55"] ["answer-efa876a3-a667-54e0-abff-4b40c1125d55" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-efa876a3-a667-54e0-abff-4b40c1125d55" :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["answer-3edc160a-8101-5d90-8138-2690348d912d" :answer/id #uuid "3edc160a-8101-5d90-8138-2690348d912d"] ["answer-3edc160a-8101-5d90-8138-2690348d912d" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-3edc160a-8101-5d90-8138-2690348d912d" :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186203333 :answer-field/correct "answer-dd2c45f0-ab57-5f4c-af72-1b497341005f"] [17592186203333 :answer-field/choices "answer-dd2c45f0-ab57-5f4c-af72-1b497341005f"] [17592186203333 :answer-field/choices "answer-23f517c8-02d6-5cbc-bbf1-171ed91219de"] [17592186203333 :answer-field/choices "answer-efa876a3-a667-54e0-abff-4b40c1125d55"] [17592186203333 :answer-field/choices "answer-3edc160a-8101-5d90-8138-2690348d912d"] [17592186204572 :question/problem "Consider the function $f:{R}^{2\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(X)={\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{x}_{ij}^{2}\n$$\n\n \nwhere ${x}_{ij}$ are the corresponding matrix's entries. What is the gradient of $f$ with respect to $X$ at $X=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]?$"] [17592186204572 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of a function $f:{R}^{m\\times n}\\to R$ with respect to a matrix $X\\in {R}^{m\\times n}$ is\n\n$$\n{∇}_{X}f(X)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{12}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{22}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{mn}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, writing out the function $f(X)$ where $X=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11} & {x}_{12} \\\\ {x}_{21} & {x}_{22}\\end{aligned}]\\in {R}^{2\\times 2}$, we get\n\n$$\nf(X)={\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{x}_{ij}^{2}=({x}_{11}^{2}+{x}_{12}^{2}+{x}_{21}^{2}+{x}_{22}^{2}).\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,{∇}_{X}f(X) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{12}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{22}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{11}}({x}_{11}^{2}+{x}_{12}^{2}+{x}_{21}^{2}+{x}_{22}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{12}}({x}_{11}^{2}+{x}_{12}^{2}+{x}_{21}^{2}+{x}_{22}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{21}}({x}_{11}^{2}+{x}_{12}^{2}+{x}_{21}^{2}+{x}_{22}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{22}}({x}_{11}^{2}+{x}_{12}^{2}+{x}_{21}^{2}+{x}_{22}^{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{11} & 2{x}_{12} \\\\ 2{x}_{21} & 2{x}_{22}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11} & {x}_{12} \\\\ {x}_{21} & {x}_{22}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2X.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $X=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{X}f(X)=[\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot (-3) & 2\\cdot (4) \\\\ 2\\cdot (1) & 2\\cdot (-2)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 8 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [17592186204572 :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate] ["answer-c0d59bb6-b177-593a-b402-558045a82d46" :answer/id #uuid "c0d59bb6-b177-593a-b402-558045a82d46"] ["answer-c0d59bb6-b177-593a-b402-558045a82d46" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-c0d59bb6-b177-593a-b402-558045a82d46" :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 2 \\\\ 8 & -4\\end{aligned}]"] ["answer-650ab510-72e3-535b-b721-0beb1e22eb23" :answer/id #uuid "650ab510-72e3-535b-b721-0beb1e22eb23"] ["answer-650ab510-72e3-535b-b721-0beb1e22eb23" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-650ab510-72e3-535b-b721-0beb1e22eb23" :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 8 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}]"] ["answer-fabfae26-7434-5570-8009-28ae299dc640" :answer/id #uuid "fabfae26-7434-5570-8009-28ae299dc640"] ["answer-fabfae26-7434-5570-8009-28ae299dc640" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-fabfae26-7434-5570-8009-28ae299dc640" :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 & -8 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]"] ["answer-3ea4ca45-df67-56c6-911d-3d71844ae6f7" :answer/id #uuid "3ea4ca45-df67-56c6-911d-3d71844ae6f7"] ["answer-3ea4ca45-df67-56c6-911d-3d71844ae6f7" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-3ea4ca45-df67-56c6-911d-3d71844ae6f7" :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]"] ["answer-4fe9b706-ba42-57d7-b61f-d9a640eeeedb" :answer/id #uuid "4fe9b706-ba42-57d7-b61f-d9a640eeeedb"] ["answer-4fe9b706-ba42-57d7-b61f-d9a640eeeedb" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-4fe9b706-ba42-57d7-b61f-d9a640eeeedb" :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 & 16 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]"] [17592186203370 :answer-field/correct "answer-650ab510-72e3-535b-b721-0beb1e22eb23"] [17592186203370 :answer-field/choices "answer-c0d59bb6-b177-593a-b402-558045a82d46"] [17592186203370 :answer-field/choices "answer-650ab510-72e3-535b-b721-0beb1e22eb23"] [17592186203370 :answer-field/choices "answer-fabfae26-7434-5570-8009-28ae299dc640"] [17592186203370 :answer-field/choices "answer-3ea4ca45-df67-56c6-911d-3d71844ae6f7"] [17592186203370 :answer-field/choices "answer-4fe9b706-ba42-57d7-b61f-d9a640eeeedb"] ["question-4c895033-c45a-5568-ba41-7003b1ce0b16" :question/id #uuid "4c895033-c45a-5568-ba41-7003b1ce0b16"] ["question-4c895033-c45a-5568-ba41-7003b1ce0b16" :question/math-academy-id "q-265980"] ["question-4c895033-c45a-5568-ba41-7003b1ce0b16" :question/problem "Consider the function $f:{R}^{2\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(X)={\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{x}_{ij}^{2}\n$$\n\n \nwhere ${x}_{ij}$ are the corresponding matrix's entries. What is the gradient of $f$ with respect to $X$ at $X=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]?$"] ["question-4c895033-c45a-5568-ba41-7003b1ce0b16" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of a function $f:{R}^{m\\times n}\\to R$ with respect to a matrix $X\\in {R}^{m\\times n}$ is\n\n$$\n{∇}_{X}f(X)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{12}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{22}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{mn}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, writing out the function $f(X)$ where $X=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11} & {x}_{12} \\\\ {x}_{21} & {x}_{22}\\end{aligned}]\\in {R}^{2\\times 2}$, we get\n\n$$\nf(X)={\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{x}_{ij}^{2}=({x}_{11}^{2}+{x}_{12}^{2}+{x}_{21}^{2}+{x}_{22}^{2}).\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,{∇}_{X}f(X) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{12}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{22}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{11}}({x}_{11}^{2}+{x}_{12}^{2}+{x}_{21}^{2}+{x}_{22}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{12}}({x}_{11}^{2}+{x}_{12}^{2}+{x}_{21}^{2}+{x}_{22}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{21}}({x}_{11}^{2}+{x}_{12}^{2}+{x}_{21}^{2}+{x}_{22}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{22}}({x}_{11}^{2}+{x}_{12}^{2}+{x}_{21}^{2}+{x}_{22}^{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{11} & 2{x}_{12} \\\\ 2{x}_{21} & 2{x}_{22}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11} & {x}_{12} \\\\ {x}_{21} & {x}_{22}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2X.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $X=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{X}f(X)=[\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot (1) & 2\\cdot (5) \\\\ 2\\cdot (-3) & 2\\cdot (4)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 10 \\\\ -6 & 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] ["question-4c895033-c45a-5568-ba41-7003b1ce0b16" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate] ["answer-field-479c37e2-c9fe-54cc-8640-7226558adb45" :answer-field/id #uuid "479c37e2-c9fe-54cc-8640-7226558adb45"] ["answer-361becea-d4d4-5a84-ac95-a90decebb407" :answer/id #uuid "361becea-d4d4-5a84-ac95-a90decebb407"] ["answer-361becea-d4d4-5a84-ac95-a90decebb407" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-361becea-d4d4-5a84-ac95-a90decebb407" :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]"] ["answer-d21d4e11-51b9-5d89-985a-ece3e742b405" :answer/id #uuid "d21d4e11-51b9-5d89-985a-ece3e742b405"] ["answer-d21d4e11-51b9-5d89-985a-ece3e742b405" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-d21d4e11-51b9-5d89-985a-ece3e742b405" :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -10 \\\\ -6 & 8\\end{aligned}]"] ["answer-881b05f3-5a11-54ed-9622-0e746ae1581b" :answer/id #uuid "881b05f3-5a11-54ed-9622-0e746ae1581b"] ["answer-881b05f3-5a11-54ed-9622-0e746ae1581b" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-881b05f3-5a11-54ed-9622-0e746ae1581b" :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 10 \\\\ -6 & 8\\end{aligned}]"] ["answer-1e4db12b-c1a2-5b3a-90cc-1eec8b8e9e29" :answer/id #uuid "1e4db12b-c1a2-5b3a-90cc-1eec8b8e9e29"] ["answer-1e4db12b-c1a2-5b3a-90cc-1eec8b8e9e29" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-1e4db12b-c1a2-5b3a-90cc-1eec8b8e9e29" :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 10 \\\\ 6 & 8\\end{aligned}]"] ["answer-8dd57312-bdfb-5065-a149-96de4be679e2" :answer/id #uuid "8dd57312-bdfb-5065-a149-96de4be679e2"] ["answer-8dd57312-bdfb-5065-a149-96de4be679e2" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-8dd57312-bdfb-5065-a149-96de4be679e2" :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 & 15 \\\\ -9 & 12\\end{aligned}]"] ["answer-field-479c37e2-c9fe-54cc-8640-7226558adb45" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-479c37e2-c9fe-54cc-8640-7226558adb45" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-479c37e2-c9fe-54cc-8640-7226558adb45" :answer-field/correct "answer-881b05f3-5a11-54ed-9622-0e746ae1581b"] ["answer-field-479c37e2-c9fe-54cc-8640-7226558adb45" :answer-field/choices "answer-361becea-d4d4-5a84-ac95-a90decebb407"] ["answer-field-479c37e2-c9fe-54cc-8640-7226558adb45" :answer-field/choices "answer-d21d4e11-51b9-5d89-985a-ece3e742b405"] ["answer-field-479c37e2-c9fe-54cc-8640-7226558adb45" :answer-field/choices "answer-881b05f3-5a11-54ed-9622-0e746ae1581b"] ["answer-field-479c37e2-c9fe-54cc-8640-7226558adb45" :answer-field/choices "answer-1e4db12b-c1a2-5b3a-90cc-1eec8b8e9e29"] ["answer-field-479c37e2-c9fe-54cc-8640-7226558adb45" :answer-field/choices "answer-8dd57312-bdfb-5065-a149-96de4be679e2"] ["question-4c895033-c45a-5568-ba41-7003b1ce0b16" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-479c37e2-c9fe-54cc-8640-7226558adb45"] [17592186204079 :knowledge-point/questions "question-4c895033-c45a-5568-ba41-7003b1ce0b16"] ["question-378ecb1a-5423-57cf-9a92-70fe30005016" :question/id #uuid "378ecb1a-5423-57cf-9a92-70fe30005016"] ["question-378ecb1a-5423-57cf-9a92-70fe30005016" :question/math-academy-id "q-265773"] ["question-378ecb1a-5423-57cf-9a92-70fe30005016" :question/problem "Consider the function $f:{R}^{2\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(X)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(det(X)).\n$$\n\n \nWhat is the gradient of $f$ with respect to $X$ at $X=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]?$"] ["question-378ecb1a-5423-57cf-9a92-70fe30005016" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of a function $f:{R}^{m\\times n}\\to R$ with respect to a matrix $X\\in {R}^{m\\times n}$ is\n\n$$\n{∇}_{X}f(X)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{12}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{22}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{mn}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, writing out the function $f(X)$ where $X=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11} & {x}_{12} \\\\ {x}_{21} & {x}_{22}\\end{aligned}]\\in {R}^{2\\times 2}$, we get\n\n$$\nf(X)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(det(X))=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}).\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,{∇}_{X}f(X) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{12}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{22}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{11}}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{12}}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{21}}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{22}}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}))\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-{x}_{22}}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}} & \\frac{{x}_{21}}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}} \\\\ \\frac{{x}_{12}}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}} & \\frac{-{x}_{11}}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $X=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{X}f(X)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{-4-(-3)} & \\frac{1}{-4-(-3)} \\\\ \\frac{-3}{-4-(-3)} & -\\frac{(-1)}{-4-(-3)}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{-1} & \\frac{1}{-1} \\\\ \\frac{-3}{-1} & -\\frac{(-1)}{-1}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] ["question-378ecb1a-5423-57cf-9a92-70fe30005016" :question/d