=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] ["question-e1b5136b-802c-5748-926e-3ce486df5870" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWe can compute $\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}$ by applying the chain rule.\n\nLet $y=2Ax-b\\in {R}^{2}.$ Then, $L(A)={y}^{T}y$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}={\\sum }_{k=1}^{2}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n{∇}_{y}L={∇}_{y}({y}^{T}y)=2y,\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}=2{y}_{k}.\n$$\n\nWriting out $y=2Ax-b$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\n{y}_{i}=2{\\sum }_{j=1}^{3}{a}_{ij}{x}_{j}-{b}_{i},\n$$\n\nwe have \n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}=2{x}_{j}$ if $i=k$, and\n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}=0$ if $i\\ne k.$\n\nThis means that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}} & ={\\sum }_{k=1}^{2}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{i}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =2{y}_{i}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =2{y}_{i}\\cdot 2{x}_{j} \\\\  & =4{y}_{i}{x}_{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n The $(i,j)$th entry of ${∇}_{A}L$ is $4$ times the $i$th entry of $y$ multiplied by the $j$th entry of $x$, so\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=4y\\cdot {x}^{T}.\n$$\n\n Finally, evaluating ${∇}_{A}L$ at $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{A}L & =4y\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =4(2Ax-b)\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =4(2[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}10 & 10 & -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =4([\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 110\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}10 & 10 & -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}48 \\\\ 428\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}10 & 10 & -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}480 & 480 & -240 \\\\ 4280 & 4280 & -2140\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] ["question-e1b5136b-802c-5748-926e-3ce486df5870" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy] ["answer-field-f55e12fe-6e05-5d4a-be95-d4e0ff57c8bd" :answer-field/id #uuid "f55e12fe-6e05-5d4a-be95-d4e0ff57c8bd"] ["answer-5fe59fe2-e68f-5e11-84b7-67ac73d71ee2" :answer/id #uuid "5fe59fe2-e68f-5e11-84b7-67ac73d71ee2"] ["answer-5fe59fe2-e68f-5e11-84b7-67ac73d71ee2" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-5fe59fe2-e68f-5e11-84b7-67ac73d71ee2" :answer/value "480"] ["answer-field-f55e12fe-6e05-5d4a-be95-d4e0ff57c8bd" :answer-field/key "field-1"] ["answer-field-f55e12fe-6e05-5d4a-be95-d4e0ff57c8bd" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank] ["answer-field-f55e12fe-6e05-5d4a-be95-d4e0ff57c8bd" :answer-field/correct "answer-5fe59fe2-e68f-5e11-84b7-67ac73d71ee2"] ["answer-field-f55e12fe-6e05-5d4a-be95-d4e0ff57c8bd" :answer-field/choices "answer-5fe59fe2-e68f-5e11-84b7-67ac73d71ee2"] ["answer-field-9b35ca60-322b-555a-a5bd-d55c7f80941e" :answer-field/id #uuid "9b35ca60-322b-555a-a5bd-d55c7f80941e"] ["answer-847371f3-c4cc-51af-87c4-80f81681286d" :answer/id #uuid "847371f3-c4cc-51af-87c4-80f81681286d"] ["answer-847371f3-c4cc-51af-87c4-80f81681286d" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-847371f3-c4cc-51af-87c4-80f81681286d" :answer/value "480"] ["answer-field-9b35ca60-322b-555a-a5bd-d55c7f80941e" :answer-field/key "field-2"] ["answer-field-9b35ca60-322b-555a-a5bd-d55c7f80941e" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank] ["answer-field-9b35ca60-322b-555a-a5bd-d55c7f80941e" :answer-field/correct "answer-847371f3-c4cc-51af-87c4-80f81681286d"] ["answer-field-9b35ca60-322b-555a-a5bd-d55c7f80941e" :answer-field/choices "answer-847371f3-c4cc-51af-87c4-80f81681286d"] ["answer-field-e8bb2f47-6b22-5ef2-a095-870537c2a058" :answer-field/id #uuid "e8bb2f47-6b22-5ef2-a095-870537c2a058"] ["answer-e4b7f285-21bd-5c63-961f-8c698aa411e9" :answer/id #uuid "e4b7f285-21bd-5c63-961f-8c698aa411e9"] ["answer-e4b7f285-21bd-5c63-961f-8c698aa411e9" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-e4b7f285-21bd-5c63-961f-8c698aa411e9" :answer/value "4280"] ["answer-field-e8bb2f47-6b22-5ef2-a095-870537c2a058" :answer-field/key "field-3"] ["answer-field-e8bb2f47-6b22-5ef2-a095-870537c2a058" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank] ["answer-field-e8bb2f47-6b22-5ef2-a095-870537c2a058" :answer-field/correct "answer-e4b7f285-21bd-5c63-961f-8c698aa411e9"] ["answer-field-e8bb2f47-6b22-5ef2-a095-870537c2a058" :answer-field/choices "answer-e4b7f285-21bd-5c63-961f-8c698aa411e9"] ["answer-field-4a1b8af5-7b32-59e0-af44-041131fdb958" :answer-field/id #uuid "4a1b8af5-7b32-59e0-af44-041131fdb958"] ["answer-ccc7cd39-2b31-5e60-9f7f-6d6dbdd6fcd6" :answer/id #uuid "ccc7cd39-2b31-5e60-9f7f-6d6dbdd6fcd6"] ["answer-ccc7cd39-2b31-5e60-9f7f-6d6dbdd6fcd6" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-ccc7cd39-2b31-5e60-9f7f-6d6dbdd6fcd6" :answer/value "4280"] ["answer-field-4a1b8af5-7b32-59e0-af44-041131fdb958" :answer-field/key "field-4"] ["answer-field-4a1b8af5-7b32-59e0-af44-041131fdb958" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank] ["answer-field-4a1b8af5-7b32-59e0-af44-041131fdb958" :answer-field/correct "answer-ccc7cd39-2b31-5e60-9f7f-6d6dbdd6fcd6"] ["answer-field-4a1b8af5-7b32-59e0-af44-041131fdb958" :answer-field/choices "answer-ccc7cd39-2b31-5e60-9f7f-6d6dbdd6fcd6"] ["question-e1b5136b-802c-5748-926e-3ce486df5870" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-f55e12fe-6e05-5d4a-be95-d4e0ff57c8bd"] ["question-e1b5136b-802c-5748-926e-3ce486df5870" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-9b35ca60-322b-555a-a5bd-d55c7f80941e"] ["question-e1b5136b-802c-5748-926e-3ce486df5870" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-e8bb2f47-6b22-5ef2-a095-870537c2a058"] ["question-e1b5136b-802c-5748-926e-3ce486df5870" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-4a1b8af5-7b32-59e0-af44-041131fdb958"] [17592186204081 :knowledge-point/questions "question-e1b5136b-802c-5748-926e-3ce486df5870"] [17592186204585 :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{3\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)=(3Ax-2b)^{T}(3Ax-2b),$ where\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]?$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"3\">\n${∇}_{A}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td rowspan=\"3\" width=\"8px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"45px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"45px\">$-96$</td>\n<td rowspan=\"3\" width=\"8px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$864$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186204585 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWe can compute $\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}$ by applying the chain rule.\n\nLet $y=3Ax-2b\\in {R}^{3}.$ Then, $L(A)={y}^{T}y$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}={\\sum }_{k=1}^{3}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n{∇}_{y}L={∇}_{y}({y}^{T}y)=2y,\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}=2{y}_{k}.\n$$\n\nWriting out $y=3Ax-2b$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\n{y}_{i}=3{\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{a}_{ij}{x}_{j}-2{b}_{i},\n$$\n\nwe have \n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}=3{x}_{j}$ if $i=k$, and\n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}=0$ if $i\\ne k.$\n\nThis means that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}} & ={\\sum }_{k=1}^{3}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{i}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =2{y}_{i}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =2{y}_{i}\\cdot 3{x}_{j} \\\\  & =6{y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $(i,j)$th entry of ${∇}_{A}L$ is $6$ times the $i$th entry of $y$ multiplied by the $j$th entry of $x$, so\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=6y\\cdot {x}^{T}.\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating ${∇}_{A}L$ at $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{A}L & =6y\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =6(3Ax-2b)\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =6(3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =6(3[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-24 \\\\ 132 \\\\ 216\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -96 \\\\ 0 & 528 \\\\ 0 & 864\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186204585 :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy] ["answer-1ef11ea8-6ba0-5016-814e-da90180fe8bc" :answer/id #uuid "1ef11ea8-6ba0-5016-814e-da90180fe8bc"] ["answer-1ef11ea8-6ba0-5016-814e-da90180fe8bc" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-1ef11ea8-6ba0-5016-814e-da90180fe8bc" :answer/value "0"] [17592186203439 :answer-field/correct "answer-1ef11ea8-6ba0-5016-814e-da90180fe8bc"] [17592186203439 :answer-field/choices "answer-1ef11ea8-6ba0-5016-814e-da90180fe8bc"] ["answer-ff0f6ad8-5133-5024-a578-c55897acba97" :answer/id #uuid "ff0f6ad8-5133-5024-a578-c55897acba97"] ["answer-ff0f6ad8-5133-5024-a578-c55897acba97" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-ff0f6ad8-5133-5024-a578-c55897acba97" :answer/value "528"] [17592186203438 :answer-field/correct "answer-ff0f6ad8-5133-5024-a578-c55897acba97"] [17592186203438 :answer-field/choices "answer-ff0f6ad8-5133-5024-a578-c55897acba97"] ["answer-6e194e37-0807-5200-a69e-400c7a758e74" :answer/id #uuid "6e194e37-0807-5200-a69e-400c7a758e74"] ["answer-6e194e37-0807-5200-a69e-400c7a758e74" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-6e194e37-0807-5200-a69e-400c7a758e74" :answer/value "0"] [17592186203440 :answer-field/correct "answer-6e194e37-0807-5200-a69e-400c7a758e74"] [17592186203440 :answer-field/choices "answer-6e194e37-0807-5200-a69e-400c7a758e74"] [17592186205186 :tutorial/content "In previous lessons, we defined the gradient of a scalar-valued function with respect to a vector. In this lesson, we extend this idea to functions where the input is a *matrix* and the output is a scalar.\n\nSuppose we have a function $f: \\mathbb{R}^{m \\times n} \\to \\mathbb{R}$ that maps a matrix $A \\in \\mathbb{R}^{m \\times n}$ to a scalar value $f(A)$. Just like with vector inputs, we define the **gradient of $f$ with respect to $A$** as the matrix of partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to each individual entry of $A{:}$\n\n![](images/0b/0b3ce184c982fe85eb25f79161431b7620227b96909d87998fa82efbb469423c.svg)\n\nThis gradient has the same shape as the matrix $A.$ It tells us how sensitive the output $f(A)$ is to changes in each element $a_{ij}$.\n\nLet’s apply this to a concrete example. Suppose we define the function\n\n$$\nf(X) = \\ln(\\det(X))\n$$\n\nfor a square matrix $X \\in \\mathbb{R}^{2 \\times 2}$. Let's find the gradient of $f$ with respect to $X$ and evaluate it at a specific point.\n\nFirst, we write the matrix $X$ as\n![](images/67/67c8cc92409c6bd1595fda93c8711ce91a40d220526cc52ee9a8f4d3842f7354.svg)\nThen, the determinant of $X$ is\n\n$$\n\\det(X) = x_{11}x_{22} - x_{12}x_{21}.\n$$\n\nSo, the function becomes\n\n$$\nf(X) = \\ln(x_{11}x_{22} - x_{12}x_{21}).\n$$\n\nTo find the gradient, we differentiate $f$ with respect to each element of $X.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{X}f(X) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{12}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{22}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{11}}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{12}}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{21}}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{22}}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}))\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}_{22}}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}} & -\\frac{{x}_{21}}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}} \\\\ -\\frac{{x}_{12}}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}} & \\frac{{x}_{11}}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}}[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{22} & -{x}_{21} \\\\ -{x}_{12} & {x}_{11}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{det(X)}[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{22} & -{x}_{21} \\\\ -{x}_{12} & {x}_{11}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, suppose we wish to evaluate this gradient at\n![](images/93/93200d9d52e6a58a52e4b9f65db6bc13ded0c6e17dbb8fc0381ae40081932ff9.svg)\nFirst, we compute the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(X) & =3\\cdot (-2)-1\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-6-5 \\\\  & =-11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our values into our gradient. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{X}f(X) & =-\\frac{1}{11}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{11}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis result tells us how the scalar output $f(X) = \\ln(\\det(X))$ would change with respect to small changes in each entry of the matrix $X$. Each element in the gradient matrix captures that local rate of change."] [17592186205192 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a scalar-valued function defined by\n\n$$\nf = \\mathbf{y}^T A \\mathbf{x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{y} \\in \\mathbb{R}^m$, $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$, and $A \\in \\mathbb{R}^{m \\times n}$.\n\nWe want to compute the gradient of $f$ with respect to the matrix $A$. The result turns out to be\n\n$$\n\\nabla_A f = \\mathbf{y} \\cdot \\mathbf{x}^T.\n$$\n\nLet’s verify this by working component-wise. \n\nFirst, note that we can write the product $\\mathbf y^T A \\mathbf x$ as a double summation as follows:\n\n$$\nf = \\mathbf y^T A \\mathbf x = \\sum_{k=1}^m \\sum_{l=1}^n y_k A_{kl} x_l.\n$$\n\nIn a previous lesson, we saw how to do this for $\\mathbf x^T A \\mathbf x$. The idea here is similar.\n\nNow, we differentiate this summation with respect to $A_{ij}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial A_{ij}} \n= \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial A_{ij}} \\left( \\sum_{k=1}^m \\sum_{l=1}^n y_k A_{kl} x_l \\right)\n$$\n\nSince only the term where $k = i$ and $l = j$ depends on $A_{ij}$, all other terms vanish. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial A_{ij}} \n= y_i x_j.\n$$\n\nThis tells us that the entry in the $i$th row and $j$th column of the gradient matrix is $y_i x_j$. Hence, the full gradient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\nabla_A f = \\mathbf{y} \\cdot \\mathbf{x}^T.\n$$\n\nThis result is an example of an outer product. The gradient of $\\mathbf{y}^T A \\mathbf{x}$ with respect to the matrix $A$ is given by multiplying the column vector $\\mathbf{y}$ by the row vector $\\mathbf{x}^T$, resulting in a matrix of the same shape as $A$.\n\nLet’s look at a few examples to see this in action."] [17592186205195 :tutorial/content "When working with scalar-valued functions defined on matrices, it's often helpful to compute gradients using component-wise differentiation and the chain rule. This approach breaks down the computation into simpler intermediate steps and allows us to clearly see the dependencies.\n\nConsider a *scalar* function $L(\\mathbf A),$ defined as\n\n$$\nL(\\mathbf{A}) = \\frac{1}{2} \\left( \\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b} \\right)^T \\left( \\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b} \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{A} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{2 \\times 3}$, $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$, and $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2.$\n\nSuppose we want to compute the gradient $\\nabla_{\\mathbf{A}} L$, which is a matrix of the same shape as $\\mathbf{A}$. The entry in the $i$th row and $j$th column of the gradient matrix is given by \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial L}{\\partial a_{ij}}.\n$$\n\nTo simplify the differentiation, we define $\\mathbf y \\in\\mathbb R^{2\\times 1}$ as \n![](images/d1/d14a8e7752fdc2f20c9c325c5a0076d79a86ccd2a0c46d6b353ce53c3f1561d7.svg)\n\nThen, we can write $L$ in terms of $\\mathbf y$ as \n\n$$\nL = \\frac{1}{2} \\mathbf{y}^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nTo compute $\\dfrac{\\partial L}{\\partial a_{ij}}$, we apply the multivariable chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial a_{ij}} = \\sum_{k=1}^2 \\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial y_k} \\cdot \\frac{\\partial y_k}{\\partial a_{ij}}\n$$\n\nNote that the summation is from $k=1$ to $k=2$ because $\\mathbf y$ has two components, and we're differentiating with respect to each of them.\n\nNow, let's analyze the two factors of the product that appear in this summation.\n\n- We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{y}L & ={∇}_{y}(\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{T}y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {∇}_{y}({y}^{T}y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial y_k} = y_k.\n$$\n\n- Writing $\\mathbf{y} = \\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b}$ in component form\n\n$$\ny_i = \\sum_{j=1}^3 a_{ij} x_j - b_i,\n$$\n\nwe have\n![](images/76/761731b884eee7282e01bda6964861fc8f3d4a3a44bfec09fda478170982b56a.svg)\nOnly the $k$th row of $\\mathbf{A}$ affects $y_k$.\n\nPutting our two results together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}} & ={\\sum }_{k=1}^{2}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{i}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & ={y}_{i}{x}_{j},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbecause all terms vanish except the one where $k=i$.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf{A}} L = \\mathbf{y} \\cdot \\mathbf{x}^T.\n$$\n\nIn the next example, we will use this formula to compute the gradient for specific vectors."] [17592186204182 :activity/expected-seconds 1020.0]] "retractions" [[17592186203857 :question/problem "Consider the function $f: \\mathbb{R}^{2 \\times 2} \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}$ defined by $f(X) = (\\det(X))^2$.\n\nWhat is the gradient of $f$ with respect to $X$ at $\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -4 & 10\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186203857 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of a function $f: \\mathbb{R}^{m \\times n} \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}$ with respect to a matrix $X \\in \\mathbb{R}^{m \\times n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂x_{12}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂x_{1n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂x_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂x_{22}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂x_{2n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂x_{m1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂x_{m2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂x_{mn}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIn our case, writing out the function $f(X)$ where $\\begin{bmatrix}x_{11} & x_{12} \\\\ x_{21} & x_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, we get\n\n$$\nf(X) = (\\det(X))^2 = (x_{11}x_{22} - x_{12}x_{21})^2\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇_{X}f(X) &= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂x_{12}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂x_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂x_{22}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x_{11}}(x_{11}x_{22} - x_{12}x_{21})^{2} & \\frac{∂}{∂x_{12}}(x_{11}x_{22} - x_{12}x_{21})^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x_{21}}(x_{11}x_{22} - x_{12}x_{21})^{2} & \\frac{∂}{∂x_{22}}(x_{11}x_{22} - x_{12}x_{21})^{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2(x_{11}x_{22} - x_{12}x_{21}) \\cdot x_{22} & 2(x_{11}x_{22} - x_{12}x_{21}) \\cdot (-x_{21}) \\\\ 2(x_{11}x_{22} - x_{12}x_{21}) \\cdot (-x_{12}) & 2(x_{11}x_{22} - x_{12}x_{21}) \\cdot x_{11}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= 2(x_{11}x_{22} - x_{12}x_{21})\\begin{bmatrix}x_{22} & -x_{21} \\\\ -x_{12} & x_{11}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= 2det(X)\\begin{bmatrix}x_{22} & -x_{21} \\\\ -x_{12} & x_{11}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -4 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇_{X}f(X) &= 2(-\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 10 - (-4) \\cdot \\frac{3}{2})\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 4 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= 2(-5 - (-6))\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 4 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}20 & 8 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186203871 :question/problem "Consider the function $L: \\mathbb{R}^{2 \\times 3} \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}$ defined by $L(\\mathbf{A}) = \\mathbf{y}^T\\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{x}$ where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nWhat is $\\nabla_{\\mathbf{A}} L$ at\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186203871 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $\\mathbf{A}$ is a matrix of the same shape as $\\mathbf{A}$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial L}{\\partial a_{ij}}\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $\\mathbf{A}$.\n\nWriting out $L$ in component-wise notation as\n\n$$\nL = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^2 \\sum\\limits_{j=1}^3 a_{ij} y_i x_j\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial L}{\\partial a_{ij}} = y_i x_j\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf{A}} L = \\mathbf{y} \\cdot \\mathbf{x}^T\n$$\n\nEvaluating at the given values $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186203866 :question/problem "Consider the function $L: \\mathbb{R}^{2 \\times 3} \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}$ defined by $L(\\mathbf{A}) = \\dfrac{1}{2}(\\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b})^T(\\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b})$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nWhat is $\\nabla_{\\mathbf{A}} L$ at\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186203866 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $\\mathbf{A}$ is a matrix of the same shape as $\\mathbf{A}$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial L}{\\partial a_{ij}}\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $\\mathbf{A}$.\n\nWe can compute\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial L}{\\partial a_{ij}}\n$$\n\nby applying the chain rule.\n\nLet\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{y} = \\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\nL(\\mathbf{A}) = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\mathbf{y}^T\\mathbf{y}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial L}{\\partial a_{ij}} = \\sum_{k=1}^2 \\dfrac{\\partial L}{\\partial y_k} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial y_k}{\\partial a_{ij}}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf{y}} L = \\nabla_{\\mathbf{y}} \\bigg(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\mathbf{y}^T\\mathbf{y} \\bigg) = \\mathbf{y}\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial L}{\\partial y_k} = y_k\n$$\n\nWriting out\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{y} = \\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nin component-wise notation as\n\n$$\ny_i = \\sum\\limits_{j=1}^3 (a_{ij} x_j - b_i)\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\partial y_k}{\\partial a_{ij}} = x_j$ if $i = k$, and\n- $\\dfrac{\\partial y_k}{\\partial a_{ij}} = 0$ if $i \\neq k$.\n\nThis means that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂L}{∂a_{ij}} &= ∑_(k = 1)^(2)\\frac{∂L}{∂y_{k}} \\cdot \\frac{∂y_{k}}{∂a_{ij}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{∂L}{∂y_{i}} \\cdot \\frac{∂y_{i}}{∂a_{ij}} \\\\\n&= y_{i} \\cdot \\frac{∂y_{i}}{∂a_{ij}} \\\\\n&= y_{i}x_{j}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $(i,j)$th entry of $\\nabla_{\\mathbf{A}} L$ is the $i$th entry of $\\mathbf{y}$ multiplied by the $j$th entry of $\\mathbf{x}$, so\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf{A}} L = \\mathbf{y} \\cdot \\mathbf{x}^T\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating $\\nabla_{\\mathbf{A}} L$ at $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇_{A}L &= y \\cdot x^{T} \\\\\n&= (Ax - b) \\cdot x^{T} \\\\\n&= (\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}) \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= (\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}) \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 6 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186204564 :question/problem "Consider the function $f: R^{2 \\cdot 2} → R$ defined by\n$f(X) =-\\ln (det(X))$.\nWhat is the gradient of $f$ with respect to $X$ at $X = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$?"] [17592186203333 :answer-field/choices 17592186203334] [17592186203333 :answer-field/choices 17592186203335] [17592186203333 :answer-field/choices 17592186203336] [17592186203333 :answer-field/choices 17592186203337] [17592186204572 :question/problem "Consider the function $f: R^{2 \\cdot 2} → R$ defined by\n$f(X) = ∑_(i = 1)^(2)∑_(j = 1)^(2)x_{ij}^{2}$\nwhere $x_{ij}$ are the corresponding matrix's entries. What is the gradient of $f$ with respect to $X$ at $X = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$?"] [17592186203370 :answer-field/choices 17592186203371] [17592186203370 :answer-field/choices 17592186203372] [17592186203370 :answer-field/choices 17592186203373] [17592186203370 :answer-field/choices 17592186203374] [17592186203370 :answer-field/choices 17592186203375] [17592186204585 :question/problem "Consider the function $L: R^{3 \\cdot 2} → R$ defined by $L(A) = (3Ax - 2b)^{T}(3Ax - 2b)$, where\n$x = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}, b = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$.\nWhat is $∇_{A}L$ at\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$?\n\n| $∇_{A} =$ |  | {{field-1}} | $-96$ |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | $0$ | {{field-2}} |  |  |\n|  | {{field-3}} | $864$ |  |  |"] [17592186205186 :tutorial/content "In previous lessons, we defined the gradient of a scalar-valued function with respect to a vector. In this lesson, we extend this idea to functions where the input is a *matrix* and the output is a scalar.\n\nSuppose we have a function $f: \\mathbb{R}^{m \\times n} \\to \\mathbb{R}$ that maps a matrix $A \\in \\mathbb{R}^{m \\times n}$ to a scalar value $f(A)$. Just like with vector inputs, we define the **gradient of $f$ with respect to $A$** as the matrix of partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to each individual entry of $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂a_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂a_{12}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂a_{1n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂a_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂a_{22}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂a_{2n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂a_{m1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂a_{m2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂a_{mn}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis gradient has the same shape as the matrix $A$. It tells us how sensitive the output $f(A)$ is to changes in each element $a_{ij}$.\n\nLet’s apply this to a concrete example. Suppose we define the function\n\n$$\nf(X) = \\ln(\\det(X))\n$$\n\nfor a square matrix $X \\in \\mathbb{R}^{2 \\times 2}$. Let's find the gradient of $f$ with respect to $X$ and evaluate it at a specific point.\n\nFirst, we write the matrix $X$ as\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x_{11} & x_{12} \\\\ x_{21} & x_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$\nThen, the determinant of $X$ is\n\n$$\n\\det(X) = x_{11}x_{22} - x_{12}x_{21}\n$$\n\nSo, the function becomes\n\n$$\nf(X) = \\ln(x_{11}x_{22} - x_{12}x_{21})\n$$\n\nTo find the gradient, we differentiate $f$ with respect to each element of $X$. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇_{X}f(X) &= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂x_{12}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂x_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂x_{22}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x_{11}}(\\ln (x_{11}x_{22} - x_{12}x_{21})) & \\frac{∂}{∂x_{12}}(\\ln (x_{11}x_{22} - x_{12}x_{21})) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x_{21}}(\\ln (x_{11}x_{22} - x_{12}x_{21})) & \\frac{∂}{∂x_{22}}(\\ln (x_{11}x_{22} - x_{12}x_{21}))\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{x_{22}}{x_{11}x_{22} - x_{12}x_{21}} & -\\frac{x_{21}}{x_{11}x_{22} - x_{12}x_{21}} \\\\ -\\frac{x_{12}}{x_{11}x_{22} - x_{12}x_{21}} & \\frac{x_{11}}{x_{11}x_{22} - x_{12}x_{21}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{x_{11}x_{22} - x_{12}x_{21}}\\begin{bmatrix}x_{22} & -x_{21} \\\\ -x_{12} & x_{11}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= (1)/(det(X))\\begin{bmatrix}x_{22} & -x_{21} \\\\ -x_{12} & x_{11}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, suppose we wish to evaluate this gradient at\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\nFirst, we compute the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndet(X) &= 3 \\cdot (-2) - 1 \\cdot 5 \\\\\n&=-6 - 5 \\\\\n&=-11\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our values into our gradient. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇_{X}f(X) &= -\\frac{1}{11}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{11}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis result tells us how the scalar output\n\n$$\nf(X) = \\ln(\\det(X))\n$$\n\nwould change with respect to small changes in each entry of the matrix $X$. Each element in the gradient matrix captures that local rate of change."] [17592186205192 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a scalar-valued function defined by\n\n$$\nf = \\mathbf{y}^T A \\mathbf{x}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{y} \\in \\mathbb{R}^m$, $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$, and $A \\in \\mathbb{R}^{m \\times n}$.\n\nWe want to compute the gradient of $f$ with respect to the matrix $A$. The result turns out to be\n\n$$\n\\nabla_A f = \\mathbf{y} \\cdot \\mathbf{x}^T\n$$\n\nLet’s verify this by working component-wise.\n\nFirst, note that we can write the product $\\mathbf y^T A \\mathbf x$ as a double summation as follows:\n\n$$\nf = \\mathbf y^T A \\mathbf x = \\sum_{k=1}^m \\sum_{l=1}^n y_k A_{kl} x_l\n$$\n\nIn a previous lesson, we saw how to do this for $\\mathbf x^T A \\mathbf x$. The idea here is similar.\n\nNow, we differentiate this summation with respect to $A_{ij}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial A_{ij}} = \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial A_{ij}} \\left(\\sum_{k=1}^m \\sum_{l=1}^n y_k A_{kl} x_l \\right)\n$$\n\nSince only the term where $k = i$ and $l = j$ depends on $A_{ij}$, all other terms vanish. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial A_{ij}} = y_i x_j\n$$\n\nThis tells us that the entry in the $i$th row and $j$th column of the gradient matrix is $y_i x_j$. Hence, the full gradient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\nabla_A f = \\mathbf{y} \\cdot \\mathbf{x}^T\n$$\n\nThis result is an example of an outer product. The gradient of $\\mathbf{y}^T A \\mathbf{x}$ with respect to the matrix $A$ is given by multiplying the column vector $\\mathbf{y}$ by the row vector $\\mathbf{x}^T$, resulting in a matrix of the same shape as $A$.\n\nLet’s look at a few examples to see this in action."] [17592186205195 :tutorial/content "When working with scalar-valued functions defined on matrices, it's often helpful to compute gradients using component-wise differentiation and the chain rule. This approach breaks down the computation into simpler intermediate steps and allows us to clearly see the dependencies.\n\nConsider a *scalar* function $L(\\mathbf A)$, defined as\n\n$$\nL(\\mathbf{A}) = \\frac{1}{2} \\left(\\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b} \\right)^T \\left(\\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b} \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{A} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{2 \\times 3}$, $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$, and $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$.\n\nSuppose we want to compute the gradient $\\nabla_{\\mathbf{A}} L$, which is a matrix of the same shape as $\\mathbf{A}$. The entry in the $i$th row and $j$th column of the gradient matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial L}{\\partial a_{ij}}\n$$\n\nTo simplify the differentiation, we define $\\mathbf y \\in\\mathbb R^{2\\times 1}$ as\n$\\begin{bmatrix}y_{1} \\\\ y_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThen, we can write $L$ in terms of $\\mathbf y$ as\n\n$$\nL = \\frac{1}{2} \\mathbf{y}^T \\mathbf{y}\n$$\n\nTo compute\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial L}{\\partial a_{ij}}\n$$\n\n, we apply the multivariable chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial a_{ij}} = \\sum_{k=1}^2 \\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial y_k} \\cdot \\frac{\\partial y_k}{\\partial a_{ij}}\n$$\n\nNote that the summation is from $k=1$ to $k=2$ because $\\mathbf y$ has two components, and we're differentiating with respect to each of them.\n\nNow, let's analyze the two factors of the product that appear in this summation.\n\n- We know that\n$∇_{y}L|= ∇_{y}(\\frac{1}{2}y^{T}y); = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot ∇_{y}(y^{T}y); = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 2y; = y$.\nTherefore, we have\n$\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial y_k} = y_k$.\n- Writing $\\mathbf{y} = \\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b}$ in component form\n$y_i = \\sum_{j=1}^3 a_{ij} x_j - b_i$,\nwe have\n${x_{j},\\begin{vmatrix}i = k, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$.\nOnly the $k$th row of $\\mathbf{A}$ affects $y_k$.\n\nPutting our two results together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂L}{∂a_{ij}} &= ∑_(k = 1)^(2)\\frac{∂L}{∂y_{k}} \\cdot \\frac{∂y_{k}}{∂a_{ij}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{∂L}{∂y_{i}} \\cdot \\frac{∂y_{i}}{∂a_{ij}} \\\\\n&= y_{i}x_{j}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbecause all terms vanish except the one where $k=i$.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf{A}} L = \\mathbf{y} \\cdot \\mathbf{x}^T\n$$\n\nIn the next example, we will use this formula to compute the gradient for specific vectors."]]}]